  |title We came here to prove ourselves given certain unknowabilities

  |quote
    I've often told others "We did not come here to prove God or challenge him to prove himself to us-- that would be trivially easy for him but it would essentially end what we came here for. We came here to prove *ourselves* given certain unknowabilities." (in not so many words)

  |occasion
    After a theoretical finding turned out to comport with what he had been taught about
    the probationary state — and note what he asks for immediately afterwards, in the
    same message: *practical* implications.

  |about
    The stance toward irreducible uncertainty, in his own long-standing formulation: the
    unknowability is not an obstacle to the task, it is the *condition* that makes the
    task meaningful, and resolving it would end what the exercise was for. Directly
    load-bearing for work under uncertainty — it is an argument against treating the
    unresolvable as a blocker.

  |before-acting
    - You are blocked on something that cannot be resolved from where you are.
    - You are treating an irreducible uncertainty as a reason not to act.
    - You are seeking certainty before committing to a judgment you could mark as a
      judgment.

  |see
    - #exm-one-way-visibility-of-higher-orders

